1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,516
A team turns on a merge queue for main on
a Friday: a waiting line that merges pull

2
00:00:09,556 --> 00:00:14,449
requests in order. On Monday there are
eleven pull requests in the queue and none

3
00:00:14,489 --> 00:00:19,566
has merged. Every one shows the same line:
a required check, a test result the rules

4
00:00:19,606 --> 00:00:22,733
demand, expected, waiting
for status to be reported.

5
00:00:22,773 --> 00:00:25,535
The same check is green
on every pull request.

6
00:00:25,875 --> 00:00:29,768
Your CTO asks: the check passed.
What is the queue waiting for?

7
00:00:30,108 --> 00:00:33,505
For a result on a commit that
no pull request run ever saw.

8
00:00:33,545 --> 00:00:38,167
The queue tests a different commit from
the one the pull request tested, and the

9
00:00:38,207 --> 00:00:43,003
workflow was never told to run there. This
video explains which commit, and why one

10
00:00:43,043 --> 00:00:47,897
extra line in the workflow fixes it. Before
that, it finishes the story of the three

11
00:00:47,937 --> 00:00:50,869
merge methods: what each
one costs you weeks later.

12
00:00:52,709 --> 00:00:56,341
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:56,381 --> 00:01:01,037
In the last video you saw what each merge
method writes at merge time: a merge

14
00:01:01,077 --> 00:01:06,034
commit that keeps your commits, a squash
that replaces them by one new commit, or a

15
00:01:06,074 --> 00:01:10,850
rebase that puts new copies in a straight
line. This video is about what happens

16
00:01:10,890 --> 00:01:12,476
afterwards, in three parts.

17
00:01:12,816 --> 00:01:18,968
Part one, section 17.9: bisect, blame,
revert and traceability under each method,

18
00:01:19,008 --> 00:01:22,867
and what your own clone
looks like after the merge.

19
00:01:22,907 --> 00:01:28,831
The replay is the second half of
labs/ch17/merge-methods.sh, in the same three

20
00:01:28,871 --> 00:01:30,360
copies as last time.

21
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:36,366
Part two, section 17.10: auto-merge, a
stored instruction on a pull request.

22
00:01:36,706 --> 00:01:41,751
Part three, section 17.11: the merge
queue, with labs/ch17/merge-queue.sh.

23
00:01:41,791 --> 00:01:45,531
In that replay the branch
prefix is the documented one.

24
00:01:45,571 --> 00:01:49,448
The rest is the textbook
author's imitation in plain Git.

25
00:01:49,488 --> 00:01:54,464
Auto-merge and the queue are described
from the documentation, with their

26
00:01:54,504 --> 00:01:59,069
availability conditions. The queue
is not walked through on GitHub.

27
00:01:59,409 --> 00:02:05,135
Labels: git revert is 🟡 CAUTION: it adds a
commit and rewrites nothing, and it moves

28
00:02:05,175 --> 00:02:09,391
the current branch and updates
the index and the working tree.

29
00:02:09,431 --> 00:02:13,442
git branch -D is 🔴 DANGEROUS,
and it gets its five answers.

30
00:02:13,482 --> 00:02:19,071
gh pr merge is 🟡 CAUTION. gh pr merge
--admin is 🔴: it merges without the required

31
00:02:19,111 --> 00:02:20,376
reviews and checks.

32
00:02:22,216 --> 00:02:23,628
After this video you can:

33
00:02:23,968 --> 00:02:27,080
Compare the three methods for
bisect, blame and revert.

34
00:02:27,420 --> 00:02:32,309
Say whether git branch -d accepts the
local branch after each method and why.

35
00:02:32,649 --> 00:02:35,136
Revert a merged pull
request under each method.

36
00:02:35,476 --> 00:02:38,510
Explain what auto-merge waits
for and what it does not check.

37
00:02:38,850 --> 00:02:43,198
Explain what a merge queue tests that
a pull request run does not, and why a

38
00:02:43,238 --> 00:02:46,200
required check can stay
unreported on queue entries.

39
00:02:48,040 --> 00:02:52,996
In one sentence: the method decides which
commits exist on main, and every later

40
00:02:53,036 --> 00:02:57,868
tool, bisect, blame, revert, an audit
that follows a commit ID, works on those

41
00:02:57,908 --> 00:03:03,051
commits and no others. Three reminders.
Bisect searches history for the commit that

42
00:03:03,091 --> 00:03:07,423
changed something. Blame names the
commit that last changed each line.

43
00:03:07,463 --> 00:03:09,984
Revert adds a commit that undoes another.

44
00:03:10,324 --> 00:03:15,331
Start from the one bit you measured last
time, with last video's branch on screen:

45
00:03:15,371 --> 00:03:19,947
is your head commit an ancestor of main?
Yes after a merge commit, as here.

46
00:03:19,987 --> 00:03:24,687
No after squash. No after rebase. After
those two the content arrived and the

47
00:03:24,727 --> 00:03:27,272
commits did not. Four observations follow.

48
00:03:27,612 --> 00:03:32,760
Observation one: git branch -d refuses.
It deletes only when the branch is merged

49
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:37,565
into its upstream, or into HEAD when
it has no upstream, and "merged" means

50
00:03:37,605 --> 00:03:42,048
reachable. Squash and rebase merges
transfer content without ancestry.

51
00:03:42,088 --> 00:03:47,109
There is nothing to prevent here. Expect
it under these two methods, and verify

52
00:03:47,149 --> 00:03:48,774
before you delete with -D.

53
00:03:49,114 --> 00:03:54,004
One caveat from the textbook: this refusal
is an inference from the documented new

54
00:03:54,044 --> 00:03:57,972
commit IDs, confirmed in the
transcript for the local equivalents.

55
00:03:58,012 --> 00:04:02,841
And the other half of the rule: while the
remote-tracking branch still exists, -d

56
00:04:02,881 --> 00:04:07,110
deletes with a warning, because the
branch is merged into its upstream.

57
00:04:07,450 --> 00:04:10,623
Observation two: blame and
log name different commits.

58
00:04:10,663 --> 00:04:15,620
After a squash, every line points to one
commit. Under squash the unit of history is

59
00:04:15,660 --> 00:04:20,202
the pull request, so the squash commit's
message must link to it, as the pull

60
00:04:20,242 --> 00:04:22,522
request number in a default title does.

61
00:04:22,862 --> 00:04:27,389
Observation three: untested commits
can land. Under merge and rebase, an

62
00:04:27,429 --> 00:04:32,526
intermediate commit of the branch is on
main, and CI tested the final result, not

63
00:04:32,566 --> 00:04:37,220
each commit. In this picture, think of
12ae95d. git bisect can stop on it.

64
00:04:37,260 --> 00:04:41,850
git bisect start --first-parent treats
a merged pull request as one step.

65
00:04:41,890 --> 00:04:46,734
For the rebase method no flag helps, and
every commit has to pass on its own.

66
00:04:46,774 --> 00:04:50,222
Squash removes the problem
and the granularity with it.

67
00:04:50,562 --> 00:04:54,101
Observation four: reverting is
three different operations.

68
00:04:54,141 --> 00:04:59,100
A merge commit needs -m 1, and carries the
trap from the merge videos: re-merging

69
00:04:59,140 --> 00:05:02,742
the branch brings nothing back
until you revert the revert.

70
00:05:02,782 --> 00:05:07,926
A squash commit reverts like any commit. A
rebased series has no marker of where the

71
00:05:07,966 --> 00:05:12,925
pull request began: one revert per commit.
On GitHub, the Revert button and gh pr

72
00:05:12,965 --> 00:05:17,368
revert create a new pull request that
reverts the original merge commit.

73
00:05:17,708 --> 00:05:23,626
Quick quiz. Your main is built with squash
merges, and one pull request must be

74
00:05:23,666 --> 00:05:28,755
undone. How many reverts? A: one,
with -m 1. B: one ordinary revert.

75
00:05:28,795 --> 00:05:31,772
C: one per original commit. Your answer?

76
00:05:36,612 --> 00:05:40,686
B. A squash commit has one parent,
so it reverts like any commit.

77
00:05:40,726 --> 00:05:43,914
A is the merge commit, and
C is the rebased series.

78
00:05:44,254 --> 00:05:48,110
No method is best. Each one
buys something and pays for it.

79
00:05:48,150 --> 00:05:53,394
Commit IDs reviewed equal commit IDs on
main: yes for a merge commit, no for the

80
00:05:53,434 --> 00:05:58,876
other two. Bisect granularity: for a merge
commit, a commit, or a pull request with

81
00:05:58,916 --> 00:06:01,716
--first-parent. For
squash, a pull request.

82
00:06:02,056 --> 00:06:07,414
For rebase, a commit. Revert: one command
with -m 1 and the re-merge trap, then one

83
00:06:07,454 --> 00:06:12,552
command, then one per commit. Reusing the
branch afterwards: safe after a merge

84
00:06:12,592 --> 00:06:15,999
commit. After a squash, old
commits are listed again.

85
00:06:16,039 --> 00:06:20,487
After a rebase the same, though a
plain rebase skips the old commits.

86
00:06:20,827 --> 00:06:25,849
The contexts the textbook gives. Merge
commits where the reviewed commit IDs must be

87
00:06:25,889 --> 00:06:30,489
the deployed ones: audits, signed-commit
policies, long-lived branches merged

88
00:06:30,529 --> 00:06:35,491
repeatedly. Squash where pull requests are
small and short-lived and commit hygiene

89
00:06:35,531 --> 00:06:40,553
inside a branch is not enforced. Rebase
where the team curates every commit, wants a

90
00:06:40,593 --> 00:06:44,289
linear history, and does not
require signatures on the target.

91
00:06:44,629 --> 00:06:49,652
Auto-merge. In one sentence: auto-merge is
a stored instruction on a pull request:

92
00:06:49,692 --> 00:06:53,294
merge with this method as soon
as every requirement is met.

93
00:06:53,634 --> 00:06:58,479
"Auto-merge merges a pull request
automatically after all required reviews and

94
00:06:58,519 --> 00:07:03,676
status checks pass." It must first be enabled
for the repository, and the option is

95
00:07:03,716 --> 00:07:07,558
shown only on pull requests that
cannot be merged immediately.

96
00:07:07,598 --> 00:07:12,693
One safety property is documented: auto-merge
is disabled if someone without write

97
00:07:12,733 --> 00:07:17,577
permissions pushes new changes to the
head branch or switches the base branch.

98
00:07:17,617 --> 00:07:22,775
Availability: public repositories on GitHub
Free, public and private on paid plans.

99
00:07:23,115 --> 00:07:27,121
What it does not check. Auto-merge
waits for required things only.

100
00:07:27,161 --> 00:07:32,210
In a repository with no required checks and
no required reviews there is nothing to

101
00:07:32,250 --> 00:07:37,054
wait for. And the documentation names one
event that switches it off, a push by

102
00:07:37,094 --> 00:07:41,653
someone without write permission. For
a commit pushed by someone with write

103
00:07:41,693 --> 00:07:46,374
permission after the approval, whether
it merges unreviewed is decided by the

104
00:07:46,414 --> 00:07:49,930
stale-approval settings from
video 124, not by auto-merge.

105
00:07:49,970 --> 00:07:54,528
Decide those settings before you allow
auto-merge on a branch that deploys.

106
00:07:54,868 --> 00:07:59,607
The merge queue. In one sentence: a merge
queue merges pull requests one group at a

107
00:07:59,647 --> 00:08:04,329
time and runs the required checks on the
exact commit that will become the new tip

108
00:08:04,369 --> 00:08:05,423
of the base branch.

109
00:08:05,763 --> 00:08:10,613
The problem it solves: without "strict"
required checks, two pull requests can each

110
00:08:10,653 --> 00:08:13,853
be green against an older
main and break main together.

111
00:08:13,893 --> 00:08:17,683
With them, every merge makes all
other pull requests out of date.

112
00:08:17,723 --> 00:08:20,393
GitHub calls that "a
race-to-merge situation".

113
00:08:20,733 --> 00:08:25,298
How it works, from the documentation: the
queue "creates temporary branches with a

114
00:08:25,338 --> 00:08:29,903
special prefix", and the changes in a pull
request "are grouped into a merge_group

115
00:08:29,943 --> 00:08:34,395
with the latest version of the base_branch
as well as changes from pull requests

116
00:08:34,435 --> 00:08:35,856
ahead of it in the queue".

117
00:08:36,196 --> 00:08:41,052
The temporary branches begin with
gh-readonly-queue/ and the base branch name, and

118
00:08:41,092 --> 00:08:45,770
"contain a different sha from the pull
request". If a group fails its checks or

119
00:08:45,810 --> 00:08:50,607
conflicts, the pull request is removed
from the queue, and the branches behind it

120
00:08:50,647 --> 00:08:52,099
are recreated without it.

121
00:08:52,439 --> 00:08:56,513
The trap. Checks on queue branches
are triggered by their own event.

122
00:08:56,553 --> 00:09:01,292
A workflow, remember, is the file that
says: when this event happens, run these

123
00:09:01,332 --> 00:09:06,192
jobs. "You must use the merge_group event
to trigger your GitHub Actions workflow

124
00:09:06,232 --> 00:09:11,214
when a pull request is added to a merge
queue ... Otherwise, status checks will not

125
00:09:11,254 --> 00:09:15,872
be triggered ... The merge will fail as
the required status check will not be

126
00:09:15,912 --> 00:09:16,477
reported."

127
00:09:16,817 --> 00:09:22,802
Also documented: the queue fixes the merge
method, and gh pr merge --admin bypasses

128
00:09:22,842 --> 00:09:23,528
the queue.

129
00:09:23,868 --> 00:09:28,398
Availability: merge queues are available
"in any public repository owned by an

130
00:09:28,438 --> 00:09:32,850
organization, or in private repositories
owned by organizations using GitHub

131
00:09:32,890 --> 00:09:35,838
Enterprise Cloud". So not
under a personal account.

132
00:09:35,878 --> 00:09:40,349
And one statement the textbook marks
unverified: the merge-queue ruleset rule

133
00:09:40,389 --> 00:09:45,270
appears only in the Enterprise Cloud view
of the documentation, and the course could

134
00:09:45,310 --> 00:09:49,839
not confirm whether a Free or Team
organization can enable the queue through a

135
00:09:49,879 --> 00:09:52,124
ruleset or only through a classic rule.

136
00:09:52,464 --> 00:09:57,208
When not to use a queue: it adds a CI
run per group and latency per merge.

137
00:09:57,248 --> 00:10:01,022
For a few merges a day, "strict"
required checks cost less.

138
00:10:02,862 --> 00:10:08,386
For the first part, keep the filing analogy
from the last video: drafts stapled in,

139
00:10:08,426 --> 00:10:13,816
one clean page retyped, or every draft
retyped. Everything in section 17.9 is the

140
00:10:13,856 --> 00:10:19,380
question "what can I still look up in the
folder?" With stapled drafts: everything,

141
00:10:19,420 --> 00:10:24,676
including the one draft that had a mistake
in it. With one clean page: only the

142
00:10:24,716 --> 00:10:30,106
final text. With retyped drafts: every
step, and no cover note saying which pages

143
00:10:30,146 --> 00:10:31,178
belong together.

144
00:10:31,518 --> 00:10:36,415
For the queue, think of a rehearsal. A pull
request's CI rehearses your change alone

145
00:10:36,455 --> 00:10:41,293
on the stage as it was. The queue rehearses
the whole evening in running order: the

146
00:10:41,333 --> 00:10:44,702
stage as it is now, plus every
act ahead of you, plus you.

147
00:10:44,742 --> 00:10:49,463
If an act ahead of you is pulled, your
rehearsal no longer describes the evening,

148
00:10:49,503 --> 00:10:50,638
and it is run again.

149
00:10:50,978 --> 00:10:55,379
The model breaks at one point: in a
theatre, a rehearsal you already did still

150
00:10:55,419 --> 00:11:00,104
happened. In the queue, a result on a commit
that will not land counts for nothing,

151
00:11:00,144 --> 00:11:02,154
because checks belong to commit IDs.

152
00:11:03,994 --> 00:11:08,419
Read the root-cause line: squash and
rebase merges transfer content without

153
00:11:08,459 --> 00:11:08,955
ancestry.

154
00:11:09,295 --> 00:11:13,323
Three approved pull requests with
their head commits on the top line.

155
00:11:13,363 --> 00:11:18,157
Below, what the queue builds: one temporary
branch per entry, each containing main

156
00:11:18,197 --> 00:11:22,991
and everything ahead of it. Compare the
IDs on the left of each line with the pull

157
00:11:23,031 --> 00:11:25,998
request heads at the top:
none of them is the same.

158
00:11:26,038 --> 00:11:29,476
The checks must run on commits
that no contributor created.

159
00:11:29,816 --> 00:11:36,288
The second entry fails. It leaves the
queue, and the third is rebuilt without it.

160
00:11:36,328 --> 00:11:41,675
Its ID changes from 0569767 to
3bda42c, so it must be tested again.

161
00:11:41,715 --> 00:11:45,695
When it passes, main moves
to exactly that commit.

162
00:11:46,035 --> 00:11:51,083
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper: copy
the queue's commit IDs from the picture,

163
00:11:51,123 --> 00:11:54,761
and tick every one that a pull
request's own run has tested.

164
00:11:54,801 --> 00:11:55,375
I'll wait.

165
00:12:00,215 --> 00:12:05,053
No ticks. And that's the hook: a workflow
that only runs on pull request events

166
00:12:05,093 --> 00:12:07,462
never runs on any line of this picture.

167
00:12:09,302 --> 00:12:13,633
You are the contributor now. The maintainer
deleted the head branch on the server,

168
00:12:13,673 --> 00:12:15,018
and you update your clone.

169
00:12:15,358 --> 00:12:19,529
Copy 1, the merge commit: -d deletes
the branch without complaint.

170
00:12:19,569 --> 00:12:21,507
Its tip is an ancestor of main.

171
00:12:23,847 --> 00:12:29,150
Copy 2, squash. Your work is on
main. What does git branch -d say?

172
00:12:29,490 --> 00:12:34,586
"Not fully merged." git branch -vv shows
the upstream as gone, so Git compares with

173
00:12:34,626 --> 00:12:37,804
HEAD and finds three commits
that are not reachable.

174
00:12:38,144 --> 00:12:41,011
Copy 3, rebase: the same refusal.

175
00:12:41,351 --> 00:12:43,964
How do you verify before -D?

176
00:12:44,304 --> 00:12:48,448
In the rebase copy, git cherry marks
every commit with a minus sign: a

177
00:12:48,488 --> 00:12:53,170
patch-equivalent commit is on main. In
the squash copy every commit gets a plus

178
00:12:53,210 --> 00:12:58,011
sign, because no single commit on main
matches. The test that works for both asks

179
00:12:58,051 --> 00:13:03,032
whether merging the branch would change main
at all: the merged tree equals the tree

180
00:13:03,072 --> 00:13:05,722
of main. The branch has
nothing left to give.

181
00:13:06,062 --> 00:13:11,015
Then the 🔴 command, git branch -D. What
it changes: the branch ref and its reflog

182
00:13:11,055 --> 00:13:16,007
are deleted. What it can destroy: nothing
at once. The commits stay in the object

183
00:13:16,047 --> 00:13:21,185
database and, if you ever had the branch
checked out, in the reflog of HEAD, for the

184
00:13:21,225 --> 00:13:24,945
retention periods. Preview: the
tree comparison you just saw.

185
00:13:24,985 --> 00:13:29,013
Recovery: git branch <name> <id>,
with the ID the command printed.

186
00:13:29,053 --> 00:13:33,451
Appropriate: after you have verified
that the base contains the content.

187
00:13:33,791 --> 00:13:39,399
Blame on the same three lines. Under the
merge commit, each line names the commit

188
00:13:39,439 --> 00:13:43,514
that introduced it. Under squash,
every line names da48bba.

189
00:13:45,854 --> 00:13:49,980
The second commit of the branch used a
wrong queue name and the third fixed it.

190
00:13:50,020 --> 00:13:54,145
Under which methods does main contain a
commit with the wrong name in its tree?

191
00:13:54,485 --> 00:13:58,457
Under merge and under rebase: one
commit each. Under squash: none.

192
00:13:58,497 --> 00:14:03,563
That intermediate commit was never tested
on its own, and git bisect can stop on it.

193
00:14:03,903 --> 00:14:04,564
Reverting.

194
00:14:04,904 --> 00:14:08,657
One revert, and you must
name the mainline with -m 1.

195
00:14:08,997 --> 00:14:10,192
One ordinary revert.

196
00:14:10,532 --> 00:14:15,003
One revert per commit, and you must know
where the pull request began: here, three

197
00:14:15,043 --> 00:14:15,719
commits back.

198
00:14:18,559 --> 00:14:22,129
Three approved one-commit pull
requests against the same main.

199
00:14:22,469 --> 00:14:27,918
The queue in order 1, 2, 3. Each temporary
branch is created from the one before and

200
00:14:27,958 --> 00:14:29,813
merges one more pull request.

201
00:14:32,153 --> 00:14:35,720
Six refs: three pull request
heads, three queue entries.

202
00:14:35,760 --> 00:14:37,653
How many different commit IDs?

203
00:14:37,993 --> 00:14:43,112
Six. The checks must run on the three queue
commits, which no pull_request run ever

204
00:14:43,152 --> 00:14:43,360
saw.

205
00:14:43,700 --> 00:14:48,853
The checks fail on the group of pull request
2. It leaves the queue, and number 3 is

206
00:14:48,893 --> 00:14:50,523
rebuilt on top of number 1.

207
00:14:50,863 --> 00:14:55,193
Here are the branch names after that:
one entry left from before, and now a

208
00:14:55,233 --> 00:14:59,912
different commit for number 3, tested
again. Predict: when its checks pass, where

209
00:14:59,952 --> 00:15:03,641
does main go, and does that commit's
ID change? Say it out loud.

210
00:15:08,481 --> 00:15:13,183
The checks pass, and the base branch moves
to the tested commit by a fast-forward,

211
00:15:13,223 --> 00:15:13,761
unchanged.

212
00:15:14,101 --> 00:15:15,762
What was tested is what landed.

213
00:15:16,102 --> 00:15:20,514
One more trigger. With it, the workflow
runs on the queue's temporary branches and

214
00:15:20,554 --> 00:15:24,966
reports the required check there. That's
what the eleven pull requests in the hook

215
00:15:25,006 --> 00:15:25,889
were waiting for.

216
00:15:26,229 --> 00:15:31,221
The first is the repository setting, once.
The second stores the instruction on pull

217
00:15:31,261 --> 00:15:33,318
request 12. The third withdraws it.

218
00:15:35,158 --> 00:15:36,806
Five mistakes to watch for.

219
00:15:37,146 --> 00:15:40,415
Enabling a merge queue without
adding the merge_group trigger.

220
00:15:40,455 --> 00:15:44,739
Root cause: checks on queue branches are
triggered by their own event; without it

221
00:15:44,779 --> 00:15:47,034
the required check is
never reported there.

222
00:15:47,374 --> 00:15:51,460
Deleting a branch with -D after
a squash merge without checking.

223
00:15:51,500 --> 00:15:56,681
Root cause: -d refused because the commits
are unreachable from main; verify that

224
00:15:56,721 --> 00:15:59,002
the base contains the content first.

225
00:15:59,342 --> 00:16:04,083
Reverting a merge commit without -m, or
re-merging the branch after the revert and

226
00:16:04,123 --> 00:16:08,982
expecting the change back. Root cause: a
merge has two parents, and a reverted merge

227
00:16:09,022 --> 00:16:11,548
is still an ancestor;
you revert the revert.

228
00:16:11,888 --> 00:16:17,038
Assuming every commit on main passed CI.
Root cause: under merge and rebase methods,

229
00:16:17,078 --> 00:16:21,733
intermediate commits land that were only
tested as part of the final result.

230
00:16:22,073 --> 00:16:26,825
Trusting auto-merge to stop an unreviewed
push. Root cause: auto-merge waits only

231
00:16:26,865 --> 00:16:31,380
for required reviews and checks; a later
push by someone with write access is

232
00:16:31,420 --> 00:16:33,747
governed by the stale-approval settings.

233
00:16:35,587 --> 00:16:40,275
Now, out of the lab. Picture three teams
in one company, with three choices, each

234
00:16:40,315 --> 00:16:42,319
defensible with this video's table.

235
00:16:42,659 --> 00:16:47,502
The payments team must show that the reviewed
commit is the deployed commit: merge

236
00:16:47,542 --> 00:16:52,444
commits, and git bisect start --first-parent
when they hunt a regression, so that a

237
00:16:52,484 --> 00:16:53,933
pull request is one step.

238
00:16:54,273 --> 00:16:59,055
The evaluation-tools team has small,
short-lived pull requests full of "fix typo"

239
00:16:59,095 --> 00:17:03,877
commits: squash, with the pull request
number in the squash message so that blame

240
00:17:03,917 --> 00:17:08,818
leads back to the discussion. They delete
head branches on merge and tell newcomers

241
00:17:08,858 --> 00:17:10,663
that git branch -d will refuse.

242
00:17:11,003 --> 00:17:15,720
The inference-runtime team curates every
commit and wants a straight line: rebase,

243
00:17:15,760 --> 00:17:20,477
on a branch that does not require signatures,
with a rule that every commit passes

244
00:17:20,517 --> 00:17:23,784
the tests on its own, because
bisect will visit each one.

245
00:17:24,124 --> 00:17:28,449
The team from the hook adds the
merge_group trigger on Monday morning.

246
00:17:28,489 --> 00:17:33,562
The eleven queued pull requests start
reporting. Then they ask the honest question

247
00:17:33,602 --> 00:17:38,676
from the end of section 17.11: at a few
merges a day, do they need a queue at all,

248
00:17:38,716 --> 00:17:41,419
or would "strict" required
checks cost less?

249
00:17:43,259 --> 00:17:47,915
Your turn. Do Exercise 22.4, "A
merge method for three teams", in

250
00:17:47,955 --> 00:17:53,912
exercises/m19-m25-github.md. For each team,
write down the requirement that decides

251
00:17:53,952 --> 00:17:59,836
the method before you name the method, and
for the method you choose, the cost the

252
00:17:59,876 --> 00:18:02,726
team accepts in bisect, blame or revert.

253
00:18:03,066 --> 00:18:08,549
The challenge is Exercise 22.7, "The
approved commit is not on main", in the same

254
00:18:08,589 --> 00:18:08,890
file.

255
00:18:10,730 --> 00:18:13,974
Question 271 of the CTO question bank:

256
00:18:16,314 --> 00:18:20,904
Answer out loud. A strong answer names
the commit each one tests and what that

257
00:18:20,944 --> 00:18:25,712
commit contains. It explains why the two
have different IDs and what follows from

258
00:18:25,752 --> 00:18:30,520
checks being attached to IDs. For the
second part it names the event, states what

259
00:18:30,560 --> 00:18:34,615
happens to the merge when the check
never reports, and gives the fix.

260
00:18:34,655 --> 00:18:37,168
It adds when a queue
is not worth its cost.

261
00:18:39,008 --> 00:18:43,875
Let's land this. You can now say what each
merge button costs weeks later, and what

262
00:18:43,915 --> 00:18:45,471
a merge queue really tests.

263
00:18:45,811 --> 00:18:47,471
You should now be able to say:

264
00:18:47,811 --> 00:18:53,670
Only a merge commit makes your commits
ancestors of main; after squash and rebase

265
00:18:53,710 --> 00:18:57,165
git branch -d refuses,
and you verify before -D.

266
00:18:57,505 --> 00:19:02,607
Squash gives coarse blame and bisect and
a one-command revert; merge commits keep

267
00:19:02,647 --> 00:19:07,367
fine steps and revert with -m 1; rebase
keeps steps and loses the grouping.

268
00:19:07,707 --> 00:19:12,196
Under merge and rebase, intermediate commits
that were never tested alone land on

269
00:19:12,236 --> 00:19:12,476
main.

270
00:19:12,816 --> 00:19:17,387
Auto-merge is a stored instruction that
waits for required reviews and checks, and

271
00:19:17,427 --> 00:19:18,343
for nothing else.

272
00:19:18,683 --> 00:19:23,101
A merge queue tests the commit that will
become the tip of the base, on temporary

273
00:19:23,141 --> 00:19:26,294
branches, and workflows must
run on the merge_group event.

274
00:19:28,134 --> 00:19:36,412
Read sections 17.9 to 17.11 of Chapter 17.
Do Exercise 22.3, "The queue that never

275
00:19:36,452 --> 00:19:43,208
finishes", and Exercise 22.6,
"Auto-merge did what it was told", in

276
00:19:43,248 --> 00:19:46,048
exercises/m19-m25-github.md.

277
00:19:46,388 --> 00:19:50,061
That closes the merge button,
from the click to the audit.

278
00:19:50,101 --> 00:19:54,478
Practise with Exercise 22.4. Next:
a Git tag versus a GitHub release.

279
00:19:54,518 --> 00:19:57,807
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

280
00:19:57,847 --> 00:19:59,344
See you in the next one.
